#import "@preview/finite:0.5.0": automaton

=== 1.

#{
  let opt = x => $(#x | emptyset)$
  let digit = $[0-9]$
  let digit-sequence = $#digit^+$
  let fractional-constant = $#digit^* "." #digit-sequence$
  let sign = $[-+]$
  let exponent-part = $["e" "E"] #opt(sign) #digit-sequence$
  let floating-suffix = $["f" "l" "F" "L"]$
  let decimal-floating-constant = $(#fractional-constant #opt(exponent-part) | #digit-sequence #exponent-part)$
  let hexadecimal-prefix = $("0x"|"0X")$
  let hexadecimal-digit = $[0-9 space "a"-"f" space "A"-"F"]$
  let hexadecimal-digit-sequence = $#hexadecimal-digit^+$
  let hexadecimal-fractional-constant = $(#hexadecimal-digit^* "." #hexadecimal-digit-sequence | #hexadecimal-digit-sequence ".")$
  let binary-exponent-part = $["p" "P"] #opt(sign) #digit-sequence$
  let hexadecimal-floating-constant = $(#hexadecimal-prefix (#hexadecimal-fractional-constant | #hexadecimal-digit-sequence) \ #binary-exponent-part )$
  let floating-constant = $(#decimal-floating-constant | \ #hexadecimal-floating-constant) \ #opt(floating-suffix)$

  floating-constant
}

Note: We use the extension for character classes, but "." matches a literal ".".
Sorry that it's so long. Maybe it's easier to read the typst code ;) .

=== 2.

With this rule, the NFSM

#figure(automaton(
  (
    q0: (qf: none),
    qf: (),
  ),
  initial: none,
  labels: (
    q0-qf: $"a" | "b"^*$,
  ),
  style: (
    q0: (label: (text: $"q"_0$)),
    qf: (label: (text: $"q"_f$)),
  ),
  final: ("qf",),
))

would expand to

#figure(automaton(
  (
    q0: (q1: "", qf: ("", "a")),
    q1: (qf: "b"),
    qf: (q1: ""),
  ),
  initial: none,
  style: (
    q0: (label: (text: $"q"_0$)),
    q1: (label: (text: $"q"_1$)),
    qf: (label: (text: $"q"_f$)),
    q1-qf: (label: (text: $"b"$)),
    qf-q1: (label: (text: $epsilon$, dist: -0.33)),
    q0-q1: (label: (text: $epsilon$), curve: 0),
    q0-qf: (label: (text: $epsilon, "a"$), curve: -2),
  ),
  final: ("qf",),
))

But this also accepts "abbbb".

=== 3.

$(r_1 | emptyset) = R_1 union emptyset = R_1$

$(r_1 epsilon) = {x.y | x in R_1, y in {epsilon}} = {x.epsilon | x in R_1} = {x | x in R_1} = R_1$

=== 4.

$X = {epsilon}$ for all $L$:

$X = L^* \\ L^+ = L^* \\ (L^* \\ L^0) = L^0 = {epsilon}$
